---
import PageLayout from '@layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL } from '@config/site';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description={`${SITE.name}'s publication standards for attribution, sourcing, corrections, commercial relationships and AI assistance.`}
  effectiveDate={LEGAL.effectiveDate}
  lastUpdated={LEGAL.lastUpdated}
>
  <p class="lead">
    This policy states the standards StackPipeline uses and the limits of its current public
    attribution. It does not invent contributor identities, qualifications or review activity
    to make the site appear more established than it is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="attribution">1. Attribution</h2>
  <p>
    Articles are currently published under the StackPipeline Editorial Team organization
    byline. No individual author profile, credential, employment history or social account is
    shown unless it is real, verifiable and approved by that contributor. Article JSON-LD uses
    the Organization as author and publisher; it does not emit Person entities or <code>sameAs</code>
    links for fictional people.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evidence-and-sourcing">2. Evidence and sourcing</h2>
  <p>
    Factual product claims should be traced to primary testing, current vendor documentation,
    a named source, or original research. A page must distinguish between an observed result,
    a vendor claim and editorial interpretation. Vendor marketing copy and AI output are not
    evidence by themselves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reviews-and-testing">3. Reviews and testing</h2>
  <p>
    A review or comparison should describe its test scope, plan or environment, date, and
    material limitations. Scores should not be published or refreshed simply to create the
    appearance of hands-on testing. The <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> is the
    standard intended for new and revised scored content.
  </p>

  <h2 id="commercial-independence">4. Commercial independence</h2>
  <p>
    Potential affiliate compensation must not determine a product's score, rank, coverage or
    editorial conclusion. Qualifying links should be disclosed clearly and marked as sponsored
    for search engines. StackPipeline currently has no configured display-advertising publisher
    ID; see the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a> for more detail.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corrections">5. Corrections and updates</h2>
  <p>
    Material updates should identify what changed and when. A page should not receive a new
    review date merely for a cosmetic edit. A public corrections process will be published when
    a verified, monitored contact channel is available; until then, the site does not claim a
    response-time commitment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-use">6. AI assistance</h2>
  <p>
    AI tools may assist with research triage, outlining or copy editing, but they are not a
    source of factual authority. Any claims, test records and citations must be reviewed before
    publication. Content should not claim a human test, editor, credential or contributor that
    does not exist.
  </p>

  <h2 id="publisher-readiness">7. Publisher readiness</h2>
  <p>
    Before applying to AdSense or enabling display ads, StackPipeline will publish a verified
    legal identity, public contact and privacy channels, a genuine publisher ID in
    <code>ads.txt</code>, and accurate public attribution. See <a href="/about/">About
    StackPipeline</a> for the current status.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
